--TEST--
An `isset(...)` operand that names no storage is refused where it is written, and parentheses are transparent — `rule:classes/unset-is-refused-on-a-property`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// PHP refuses the identical shape at compile time and its own message names
// the replacement: `isset` asks whether *storage* holds something, and an
// operand that is already a value has nothing to ask that `rule:classes/unset-is-refused-on-a-property`'s own
// definition — `$x != null` — does not ask more plainly.
final class Reg {
    public const string NAME = "n";
    public static function tag(): ?string {
        return null;
    }
}
string $s = "x";
echo isset(Reg::tag()) ? "y" : "n", "\n";
echo isset($s . "z") ? "y" : "n", "\n";
echo isset(Reg::NAME) ? "y" : "n", "\n";

// Every operand of a list is judged on its own, so one bad one in a
// conjunction is one report and the others still stand.
echo isset($s, Reg::tag()) ? "y" : "n", "\n";

// Parentheses are transparent, exactly as they are in PHP: `isset(($s))` is
// `isset($s)`, and neither is refused.
echo isset(($s)) ? "y" : "n", "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
